# The Justice Party Has Become the Dravidar Kazhagam
Tamil title: ஜஸ்டிஸ்கட்சி திராவிடர் கழகமாகிவிட்டது

Special Correspondent

## Resolution to Relinquish Titles and Offices

P. Balasubramaniam's humility; K. A. P. Viswanatham's graciousness

The sixteenth conference of the Justice Party was splendidly held in the Thiagarayar pavilion at Salem on 27 August 1944. The conference president, Periyar E. V. Ramasamy, arrived that morning at Salem Market railway station. The cheering of the Periyar Volunteer Corps, tens of thousands of young people from Dravida Nadu, commanders and worker comrades shouting “Long live Periyar!” may truly be said to have split the sky.

The leader was taken by motorcar to Comrade R. S. Arunachalam's home. Later, Periyar and Commander Pandian were seated in a large ceremonial carriage. Surrounded by comrades including K. A. P. Viswanatham and P. Balasubramaniam, many thousands began a great procession with cries of “Long live Periyar!” and “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians!”

Two elephants and volunteers carrying flags upon fifty horses followed the leader in two divisions. Volunteers at the front bore upon an elephant a flag depicting a map of Dravida Nadu. These sights appeared to place a crown upon the procession.

It is impossible to describe in writing the scenes of people all along the route garlanding Periyar in abundance and cheering. Crowds joined until the procession extended for about a mile. Members of the Periyar Volunteer Corps from Golden Rock, including Golden Rock workers, and Tuticorin mill workers marched with red flags, shouting “Periyar alone is our leader” and distributing printed leaflets. The procession reached the conference pavilion at 11:30 a.m.

The pavilion was built to accommodate more than ten thousand people and decorated attractively. On its large stage hung great portraits of T. M. Nair, Sir P. Theagaraya Chetty, the Raja of Panagal and Sir A. T. Pannirselvam. To everyone who saw them, they seemed alive and present. A large depiction of the scales flag hung behind the stage.

Approximately 3,500 delegates and 7,000 observers filled the enclosure like a surging flood. With loudspeakers, those for whom there was no space listened in groups outside and along the streets.

Secretary Comrade C. G. Netto requested everyone to remain calm and make the conference a success. If there were shortcomings in its arrangements, he asked visiting comrades to overlook them and remain united for the shared aim of obtaining Dravida Nadu. He then invited Comrade P. Balasubramaniam to hoist the Justice Party flag.

After raising it, Balasubramaniam addressed the respected leader and comrades. He regretted claims that a difference existed between himself and the leader, saying he had always followed Periyar's policy. Nothing, he said, could equal the sacrifices made by their incomparable leader for the country's self-respect.

He had once believed that the Raja of Bobbili and Sir R. K. Shanmukham Chetty could lead the party, but their actions had ended in betrayal. No one would accept those leaders who contributed to the Kasturba Fund. Periyar alone was the unique great leader of Dravida Nadu. Only he could awaken the feeling needed both to obtain Dravidastan and to acquire the self-respect required to administer it.

Periyar should become the first leader of the republican government of the future Dravidastan. In brief, Balasubramaniam called him the Karl Marx of Dravida Nadu, a revolutionary in religion, society, economics and politics. He had realized that Dravidastan could be won only under this incomparable freedom fighter.

He had never opposed Periyar; his desire was to strengthen the party. Before the many thousands present, he pledged always to serve under Periyar's leadership. While Periyar was imprisoned, they had safeguarded the party flag. Remaining disciplined beneath it today and hereafter, they would obtain national liberation under Periyar. He raised the flag and declared that Periyar should be appointed dictator of their country.

Opening the conference, K. A. P. Viswanatham addressed Periyar, the women and the comrades. He had remained apart for three years, he said, not from hostility toward Periyar but from a desire to strengthen the party. Nothing could equal Periyar's service. If the government did not respect the party, they must teach it a proper lesson, and the party organization had to become strong enough to do so.

In his humble opinion, Periyar should become not merely party leader but dictator of the struggle for national liberation. They must be prepared to obey his commands and live in unity. Viswanatham had previously harboured some doubt about the separation of Dravida Nadu, but now understood its necessity. He called on the gathering to serve courageously under Periyar, eradicate Aryanism, the obstacle to their liberation, and achieve a separate Dravida Nadu.

## Address of the Reception President

Reception president Comrade Ratnasami welcomed everyone. He said that in the coming struggle for Dravidian awakening all must work with discipline and expel Aryanism from the country. Periyar alone possessed every distinction needed to lead and assist them. This conference would be notable in the movement's history. He explained these points at length and requested cooperation despite any faults in the arrangements.

## President Periyar's Clarion Call

At the request of C. G. Netto and with support from Soundarapandian, T. Shanmugam, Duraisami, Ganesha Sankar and Mrs Ramamirthammal, Periyar was elected conference president amid applause and valiant cries of “Long live Periyar!” and “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians!”

Periyar said he was somewhat pleased by the election and even more moved by the proud affection shown when he entered the hall. This would be his final term as president of the Justice Party; at the next conference they must elect another leader.

He knew they sincerely wanted him to continue. In obedience to his call, many thousands of young people had assembled from across the country. When he requested that they avoid disturbance, they had respected his wishes and remained disciplined.

P. Balasubramaniam had said that he always followed the leader, that no one else was qualified to lead, and praised Periyar extravagantly in several ways. Setting other matters aside in light of Balasubramaniam's present approach, Periyar welcomed him. His friend Viswanatham likewise said there had been no mental bitterness between them, called for party organization and suggested that making the leader dictator would be best.

Periyar answered that, from the time he entered public life, he had always possessed something of a dictatorial disposition. If the people in their present state were to gain even a measure of self-respect, the leader who assumed responsibility could accomplish nothing, however small, without such a disposition.

He knew proposals had been made about party organization. It was madness to imagine that the government would respect them merely because an organizational system existed. That did not mean he opposed organization. But he asked its critics what they had achieved in their own districts. As party leader, he had published many appeals, written letters repeatedly, made personal requests and even appointed paid organizers in some districts. What happened? How many leaders took part? In how many districts were party programmes attended to? Did they believe people would forget their failures merely because they offered empty philosophy and complained that the party lacked organization or that this and that should be done differently?

Consider Congress, he said. Did Achariyar or Gandhi travel from town to town establishing associations? Did Mr Jinnah? When they issued a statement, people everywhere joined and acted. In this movement, however, everything proceeded as intellectual display and upside down, while people went about claiming no organization had been created. That argument could not be accepted.

The government would never be alarmed by party organization. It might be useful to those who demanded organization because they desired offices through it. But to compel the government to accept their purpose, at least four thousand people must be ready for prison.

They must present the country's true needs with discipline and conduct just agitation; only then would the government's eyes open. If instead they coveted dishonourable titles and offices, prostrated themselves a thousand and eight times at the government's feet and waited at the Governor's palace for an interview, any organization they possessed would be counterfeit. Government and other movements would regard them and their organization with contempt. Within a few days, they must be ready to make a great sacrifice.

Periyar knew that his beloved friend Soundarapandian, Comrade Shanmugam of Tiruvottiyur and one or two others regarded their positions as trivial, gained not even a speck of benefit from them and were ready to throw them away upon that stage. At times Periyar spoke angrily of Pandian only because some people used his name to invent things and create needless trouble; he wanted those people to learn sense. Pandian knew this. Periyar treated him like a brother, while Pandian regarded Periyar as a father or elder brother. Of the few trustworthy people capable of leading the party properly in his place, Pandian stood foremost.

The movement must now begin its agitation intensely, teach Aryanism a lesson and compel the government, through that agitation, to fulfil the desire for Dravida Nadu's liberation. If anything Periyar had said appeared severe, it was solely to develop discipline among them.

The conference adjourned for lunch and reconvened at 3:30 p.m. From 3:30 until 5:00, the Subjects Committee debated important resolutions. Commander Pandian, T. Shanmugam and many comrades participated, while Periyar and another comrade whose initials are damaged in the source explained the resolutions.

At five o'clock, resolutions were placed before the conference. C. N. Annadurai moved that the name South Indian Liberal Federation be replaced by Dravidar Kazhagam; T. Shanmugam supported it. Netto, Ganesha Sankar and Madurai Venugopal opposed it. When agitation threatened in the gathering, Periyar calmed it, saying their arguments could be answered appropriately. Many came forward to support the motion, but owing to lack of time the leader himself replied.

Periyar said there had never actually been a party called the Justice Party: only its English newspaper had borne the name *Justice*. Since they did not accept the name “Indians,” South Indian Liberal Federation was being changed to Dravidar Kazhagam. The cry that adequate notice had not been given was trickery. At an executive meeting in Salem eight months earlier, Netto, who now opposed it, had himself supported it. Amid cries of “Shame!”, Periyar put the resolution to a vote.

Tens of thousands raised their hands with cheers of “Long live the Dravidar Kazhagam!” The president asked opponents to raise their hands. Even those who had spoken against it stood silently without doing so, provoking renewed cries of “Shame!” Netto immediately left the gathering.

Annadurai's resolution that members relinquish titles and offices bestowed by the government was introduced with Commander Pandian's support and an amendment allowing six months. Many delegates demanded immediate relinquishment. The leader explained the reasons for delay, and it was finally resolved to establish branches throughout the country and implement the measure at a special conference within six months.

Election of the party's new executive committee was deferred until March 1945. A resolution was passed requiring workers to receive a share in profits.

Regional leaders were selected to establish associations throughout the country by 31 March 1945. W. P. A. Soundarapandian was assigned Madurai, Ramanathapuram and Tirunelveli; K. K. Neelamegam was assigned Tiruchirappalli, Thanjavur and South Arcot. The remaining names and regions are heavily damaged in the source, though Chennai, Chingleput, North Arcot, Salem, Coimbatore and the name E. V. Krishnasami remain partly visible.

After many other resolutions, Salem Duraisami offered thanks and the conference ended pleasantly.

It would be no exaggeration to say that Salem had acted with distinction. Only nine people, including Netto, Ganesha Sankar and P. Balasubramaniam, signed and submitted an objection to the change of party name. They relied upon the stale old refrain that adequate notice had not been given. It was also said that they collected donations intending to conduct the party under its old name.

3 September 1944.

---NOTES---
The scans confirm the title `ஜஸ்டிஸ்கட்சி திராவிடர் கழகமாகிவிட்டது`, the conference date `27-8-44`, and the closing date `3-9-1944`. OCR had damaged the title's first word and rendered the conference date as `87-8-48`; the two canonical Tamil copies were corrected before building this file.

The report's subtitle contains abbreviated names whose OCR is uneven. `பி.பா.` is rendered as P. Balasubramaniam from the report, and `கி.ஆ.பெ.` is understood contextually as K. A. P. Viswanatham. Several other initials remain too degraded for responsible expansion.

The final regional-leader list is seriously damaged in OCR. The English translates only entries and place names that remain recoverable, explicitly marking the omitted portion instead of inventing names. The complete corrected Tamil OCR block is preserved verbatim below.

`சர்வாதிகாரி` is translated literally as “dictator,” reflecting the historical wording of the speeches. `தெ.இ.ந.ல. சங்கம்` refers to the South Indian Liberal Federation, the formal name associated with the Justice Party. Political and ethnic terms retain the report's 1944 usage.
